printf("%s\n", buf);
```

Your friend is right. `"hello"` needs **6 bytes**: 5 letters plus the `'\0'` terminator. `buf` has 5, so `strcpy` writes one byte past the end of the array. This is a **buffer overflow**, which is undefined behaviour. "Works on my machine" just means the extra byte overwrote something that didn't matter *this time*. With different compiler settings it can corrupt other variables or crash. Overflows like this are also a classic security hole.

Fix options:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* 1. Make the buffer big enough */
    char a[6];
    strcpy(a, "hello");

    /* 2. Let the compiler size it */
    char b[] = "hello";

    /* 3. Bounded copy that always terminates and reports truncation */
    char c[5];
    int n = snprintf(c, sizeof c, "%s", "hello");
    if (n >= (int)sizeof c)
        printf("truncated to \"%s\"\n", c);   /* "hell" */

    printf("%s %s\n", a, b);
    return 0;
}
```

Steps to avoid this in future:
1. Always count the `'\0'`: a buffer needs `strlen(s) + 1` bytes.
2. Prefer `snprintf(dst, sizeof dst, "%s", src)` over `strcpy` when the source length is unknown.
3. Build test programs with `-fsanitize=address`. It reports this exact bug at runtime.

Key points:
- Undefined behaviour that "works" is still a bug.

A **queue** is first-in, first-out (FIFO). A **circular buffer** is a fixed array where the read and write positions wrap back to index 0 when they reach the end, so freed slots get reused without shifting any data.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define QCAP 4

struct queue {
    int data[QCAP];
    size_t head;   /* index of the oldest item */
    size_t count;  /* number of items stored */
};

bool enqueue(struct queue *q, int v)
{
    if (q->count == QCAP)
        return false;                        /* full */
    size_t tail = (q->head + q->count) % QCAP;
    q->data[tail] = v;
    q->count++;
    return true;
}

bool dequeue(struct queue *q, int *out)
{
    if (q->count == 0)
        return false;                        /* empty */
    *out = q->data[q->head];
    q->head = (q->head + 1) % QCAP;          /* wrap around */
    q->count--;
    return true;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct queue q = { .head = 0, .count = 0 };
    int v;

    for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) enqueue(&q, i);   /* 1 2 3 4 */
    dequeue(&q, &v); printf("%d\n", v);            /* 1 */
    dequeue(&q, &v); printf("%d\n", v);            /* 2 */
    enqueue(&q, 5);                                /* wraps to index 0 */
    enqueue(&q, 6);
    if (!enqueue(&q, 7)) printf("full\n");

    while (dequeue(&q, &v)) printf("%d ", v);      /* 3 4 5 6 */
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

How it works:
1. `head` is where the next item will be read from.
2. The write position is `(head + count) % QCAP`. `%` (remainder) is what makes the index wrap.
3. Keeping a `count` makes "full" and "empty" easy to tell apart. With only head and tail indexes, both states look like `head == tail`.

Key points:
- Every operation is O(1).
- This version is not thread-safe. Two threads using it at once need a lock.

My program crashes with "free(): double free detected". Here is the code:

```c
char *name = malloc(20);
strcpy(name, "sam");
free(name);
if (strlen(name) > 0)
    free(name);
```

It has two bugs, both caused by using `name` after it was freed.

1. **Use after free:** `strlen(name)` reads memory you already gave back. After `free`, the pointer still holds the old address, but that memory no longer belongs to you.
2. **Double free:** you call `free(name)` a second time on the same block. This corrupts the allocator's internal bookkeeping. glibc detects it and aborts, which is the message you saw.

Fixed version:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    char *name = malloc(20);
    if (name == NULL)
        return 1;
    strcpy(name, "sam");
    printf("%zu\n", strlen(name));   /* use it BEFORE freeing */

    free(name);
    name = NULL;                     /* mark it as gone */

    free(name);                      /* free(NULL) is defined to do nothing */
    return 0;
}
```

Steps:
1. Finish every use of the memory before you `free` it.
2. Set the pointer to `NULL` right after `free`. A later accidental `free` then does nothing, and a later dereference crashes immediately instead of silently reading stale data.
3. Make it clear which part of the code **owns** each allocation (is responsible for freeing it), so exactly one place frees it.

Key points:
- Compile with `-fsanitize=address -g`. AddressSanitizer reports the exact line of a use-after-free.
- Setting the pointer to `NULL` only protects that one variable. Copies of the pointer still dangle.

Explain this code, I don't get why it works:

```c
