printf("%d %d %d\n", *(arr + 1), arr[2], 2[arr]);
```

It prints `20 30 30`. All three expressions use the same rule.

1. **`arr + 1`**: `arr` decays to a pointer to `arr[0]`. Adding 1 to a pointer moves it forward by **one element**, not one byte. For `int` that is `sizeof(int)` bytes (usually 4). This is **pointer arithmetic**.
2. **`*(arr + 1)`**: dereferences that address, giving `arr[1]`, which is 20.
3. **`arr[2]`**: the C standard *defines* `a[i]` as `*(a + i)`, so this is `*(arr + 2)`, which is 30.
4. **`2[arr]`**: by the same definition this is `*(2 + arr)`. Addition is commutative, so it's identical to `*(arr + 2)`, which is 30.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30};
    int *p = arr;
    printf("%d %d %d\n", *(arr + 1), arr[2], 2[arr]);   /* 20 30 30 */
    printf("%td\n", (p + 2) - p);                       /* 2 (elements) */
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- `2[arr]` is legal but confusing. Never write it in real code.
- Subtracting two pointers into the same array gives a distance in elements (type `ptrdiff_t`, printed with `%td`).

There are two common ways.

**Option A: one contiguous block** (recommended). One `malloc`, one `free`, and good cache use. You compute the index yourself.

**Option B: array of row pointers.** You can write `m[r][c]`, but it needs `rows + 1` allocations and more cleanup code.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    size_t rows = 3, cols = 4;   /* imagine these came from scanf */

    /* Option A: single block, index = r * cols + c */
    int *grid = calloc(rows * cols, sizeof *grid);
    if (grid == NULL) return 1;
    grid[1 * cols + 2] = 7;
    printf("A: %d\n", grid[1 * cols + 2]);
    free(grid);

    /* Option B: row pointers */
    int **m = malloc(rows * sizeof *m);
    if (m == NULL) return 1;
    for (size_t r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
        m[r] = calloc(cols, sizeof *m[r]);
        if (m[r] == NULL) {                 /* undo partial work */
            for (size_t k = 0; k < r; k++) free(m[k]);
            free(m);
            return 1;
        }
    }
    m[1][2] = 9;
    printf("B: %d\n", m[1][2]);
    for (size_t r = 0; r < rows; r++) free(m[r]);
    free(m);
    return 0;
}
```

Steps for option A:
1. Allocate `rows * cols` elements.
2. Access element (r, c) as `grid[r * cols + c]`.
3. Free it once.

Key points:
- `calloc` zeroes the memory and checks the `rows * cols` multiplication for overflow.
- In option B, free each row before freeing `m`. Freeing in the wrong order leaks the rows.

Walk the list once and flip each node's `next` pointer so it points backwards. You need three pointers: `prev`, `cur` and `next`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node { int v; struct node *next; };

struct node *reverse(struct node *head)
{
    struct node *prev = NULL;
    struct node *cur = head;
    while (cur != NULL) {
        struct node *next = cur->next;  /* 1. remember the rest */
        cur->next = prev;               /* 2. flip the link */
        prev = cur;                     /* 3. move prev forward */
        cur = next;                     /* 4. move cur forward */
    }
    return prev;                        /* old tail is the new head */
}

int main(void)
{
    struct node c = {3, NULL}, b = {2, &c}, a = {1, &b};   /* 1->2->3 */
    struct node *head = reverse(&a);
    for (struct node *p = head; p; p = p->next)
        printf("%d ", p->v);            /* 3 2 1 */
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- O(n) time and O(1) extra memory: it uses no new nodes.
- Always save `cur->next` before overwriting it, or you lose the rest of the list.
- The nodes here are local variables to keep the demo short. Real lists use `malloc`.

Yes. The idea: reverse everything after the head, then attach the head at the end.

```c
/* partial snippet: uses struct node from above */
struct node *reverse_rec(struct node *head)
{
    if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL)
        return head;                        /* 0 or 1 node: already reversed */
    struct node *new_head = reverse_rec(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;                /* node after head now points back */
    head->next = NULL;                      /* head becomes the tail */
    return new_head;
}
```

Steps for the list `1->2->3`:
1. `reverse_rec(1)` calls `reverse_rec(2)`, which calls `reverse_rec(3)`.
2. `3` is the last node, so it is returned as `new_head`.
3. Back in the call for `2`: `3->next = 2` and `2->next = NULL`.
4. Back in the call for `1`: `2->next = 1` and `1->next = NULL`. The result is `3->2->1`.

Key points:
- Each node adds one stack frame, so a list of millions of nodes can overflow the stack. Prefer the loop version in production code.

Use `fgets`.

- `scanf("%s", buf)` stops at the first space (so "Ana Lee" becomes "Ana"), and it has **no length limit**, so long input overflows `buf`.
- `fgets(buf, size, stdin)` reads a whole line, never writes more than `size` bytes, and always terminates the string. It keeps the trailing `'\n'`, which you remove yourself.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    char name[32];
    printf("Name: ");
    if (fgets(name, sizeof name, stdin) == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "no input\n");
        return 1;
    }
    name[strcspn(name, "\n")] = '\0';   /* strip newline if present */
    printf("Hello, %s!\n", name);
    return 0;
}
```

Steps:
1. Call `fgets` with `sizeof name` as the limit.
2. Check for `NULL`, which means end of input or an error.
3. `strcspn(name, "\n")` returns the index of the first `'\n'` (or the string length if there is none). Writing `'\0'` there removes the newline.

Key points:
- If you must use `scanf`, give it a width: `scanf("%31s", name)` for a 32-byte buffer.
- To read a number safely, read a line with `fgets` and then parse it with `strtol`.

Review this, my second user's name changes when I edit the first:

```c
struct user { char *name; int id; };
struct user a, b;
a.name = malloc(16);
strcpy(a.name, "alice");
b = a;
strcpy(a.name, "bob");
printf("%s\n", b.name);  // prints bob??
```

`b = a;` copies the struct **member by member**. `name` is a pointer, so only the *address* is copied, not the text. Both `a.name` and `b.name` now point to the same 16 bytes. This is a **shallow copy**. When you change the text through `a`, `b` sees the change.

It also causes a second problem: if you later `free(a.name)` and `free(b.name)`, you free the same block twice.

The fix is a **deep copy**: give `b` its own buffer.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

struct user { char *name; int id; };

int user_copy(struct user *dst, const struct user *src)
{
    dst->name = malloc(strlen(src->name) + 1);
    if (dst->name == NULL) return -1;
    strcpy(dst->name, src->name);
    dst->id = src->id;
    return 0;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct user a = { .id = 1 }, b;
    a.name = malloc(16);
    if (a.name == NULL) return 1;
    strcpy(a.name, "alice");

    if (user_copy(&b, &a) != 0) { free(a.name); return 1; }
    strcpy(a.name, "bob");
    printf("a=%s b=%s\n", a.name, b.name);   /* a=bob b=alice */

    free(a.name);
    free(b.name);
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- Struct assignment copies pointers, never the data they point to.
- Each `malloc` must have exactly one matching `free`.

- **`extern`** in a header **declares** a variable: "it exists somewhere". It doesn't create it.
- Exactly **one** `.c` file **defines** it (creates it, optionally with a starting value).
- **`static`** on a global or a function gives it **internal linkage**: only that `.c` file can see it. Other files can't access it, even with `extern`.

```c
// counter.h
#ifndef COUNTER_H
#define COUNTER_H

extern int g_calls;      /* declaration: shared with everyone */
void counter_tick(void);
int counter_private_total(void);

#endif
```

```c
// counter.c
#include "counter.h"

int g_calls = 0;         /* the one definition */
static int total = 0;    /* private to this file */

void counter_tick(void)
{
    g_calls++;
    total += 10;
}

int counter_private_total(void)
{
    return total;
}
```

```c
// main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "counter.h"

int main(void)
{
    counter_tick();
    counter_tick();
    printf("calls=%d total=%d\n", g_calls, counter_private_total());
    return 0;
}
```

Build: `cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra main.c counter.c -o app`. Output: `calls=2 total=20`.

Key points:
- Define a variable in a header (`int g_calls = 0;` in the `.h`) and you get "multiple definition" link errors once two files include it.
- Make globals and helper functions `static` by default. Expose only what other files need.

In a **doubly linked list**, each node has a `prev` pointer and a `next` pointer. To delete a node, connect its neighbours to each other, update the head if needed, then free the node.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct dnode {
    int v;
    struct dnode *prev, *next;
};

void dlist_remove(struct dnode **head, struct dnode *n)
{
    if (n->prev) n->prev->next = n->next;  /* left neighbour skips n */
    else         *head = n->next;          /* n was the head */
    if (n->next) n->next->prev = n->prev;  /* right neighbour skips n */
    free(n);
}

int dlist_push_front(struct dnode **head, int v)
{
    struct dnode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (!n) return -1;
    n->v = v;
    n->prev = NULL;
    n->next = *head;
    if (*head) (*head)->prev = n;
    *head = n;
    return 0;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct dnode *head = NULL;
    for (int i = 4; i >= 1; i--)
        if (dlist_push_front(&head, i)) return 1;   /* 1 2 3 4 */

    dlist_remove(&head, head->next);   /* remove 2 (middle) */
    dlist_remove(&head, head);         /* remove 1 (head) */

    for (struct dnode *p = head; p; p = p->next)
        printf("%d ", p->v);           /* 3 4 */
    printf("\n");

    while (head) dlist_remove(&head, head);   /* free the rest */
    return 0;
}
```

Steps:
1. If `n` has a previous node, set `prev->next = n->next`. Otherwise `n` is the head, so move the head to `n->next`.
2. If `n` has a next node, set `next->prev = n->prev`.
3. Call `free(n)`. Do this last, after you've finished reading its fields.

Key points:
- Deleting a node you already hold a pointer to is O(1). In a singly linked list you'd first have to search for the previous node.
- `head` is passed as `struct dnode **` because removing the first node changes it.

A **dispatch table** maps a key (here, an operator character) to the function that handles it. It replaces a long `switch` and makes adding operations easy.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

typedef double (*binop)(double, double);   /* pointer-to-function type */

static double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
static double sub(double a, double b) { return a - b; }
static double mul(double a, double b) { return a * b; }
static double divide(double a, double b) { return a / b; }

struct op {
    char symbol;
    binop fn;
};

static const struct op ops[] = {
    { '+', add }, { '-', sub }, { '*', mul }, { '/', divide },
};

static binop find_op(char c)
{
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof ops / sizeof ops[0]; i++)
        if (ops[i].symbol == c)
            return ops[i].fn;
    return NULL;
}

int main(void)
{
    const char tests[] = { '+', '*', '/', '%' };
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof tests; i++) {
        binop f = find_op(tests[i]);
        if (f == NULL)
            printf("8 %c 2: unknown operator\n", tests[i]);
        else
            printf("8 %c 2 = %g\n", tests[i], f(8, 2));   /* call through pointer */
    }
    return 0;
}
```

How it works:
1. `typedef double (*binop)(double, double);` names the type "pointer to a function taking two doubles and returning a double". The `typedef` keeps the rest of the code readable.
2. Each table entry pairs a symbol with a function name. A function name used as a value turns into its address.
3. `find_op` searches the table and returns the matching pointer, or `NULL`.
4. `f(8, 2)` calls whichever function `f` points to.

Key points:
- Always check a function pointer for `NULL` before calling it.
- Interpreters, state machines, plugin systems and `qsort` comparators all use this pattern.

A running C program keeps data in three main kinds of storage. They differ in **lifetime**: how long the memory stays valid.

1. **Stack (automatic storage)**
   - Holds local variables and function parameters.
   - Created when the function is called, and gone when it returns.
   - Fast, but small (often 1-8 MB in total). Large arrays here can cause a stack overflow.
2. **Heap (allocated storage)**
   - Memory you request with `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc`.
   - Lives until you call `free`. You control its lifetime.
   - Large, but you must free it, or it leaks.
3. **Static storage**
   - Globals, `static` variables, and string literals.
   - Exists for the whole run of the program. Variables without an initialiser start at zero.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int counter;                   /* static storage, starts at 0 */

int main(void)
{
    int local = 5;             /* stack */
    static int calls = 0;      /* static storage, despite being inside main */
    int *heap = malloc(sizeof *heap);   /* heap */
    if (heap == NULL)
        return 1;
    *heap = 42;
    calls++;
    printf("%d %d %d %d\n", counter, local, calls, *heap);
    free(heap);                /* heap memory ends here */
    return 0;
}                              /* stack memory for local ends here */
```

Key points:
- Rule of thumb: a pointer is only valid while the object it points to is alive. Returning the address of a stack variable breaks that rule.
- The standard doesn't use the words "stack" and "heap". It says "automatic" and "allocated". In practice, though, mainstream implementations work as described above.

I think my program leaks memory. How do I find where?

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    char *line = malloc(256);
    snprintf(line, 256, "item %d", i);
    puts(line);
}
```

Yes, it leaks. Each loop iteration allocates 256 bytes and never frees them, so 1000 blocks (256 KB) are lost. When you overwrite `line` in the next iteration, the previous address is gone forever.

Fix: free each block at the end of the iteration (and check `malloc`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        char *line = malloc(256);
        if (line == NULL)
            return 1;
        snprintf(line, 256, "item %d", i);
        if (i % 250 == 0)
            puts(line);
        free(line);          /* release before the pointer goes away */
    }
    return 0;
}
```

To find leaks in general:
1. **Valgrind (Linux):** build with `-g`, then run `valgrind --leak-check=full ./prog`. It lists each leaked block with the line that allocated it.
2. **AddressSanitizer:** build with `cc -g -fsanitize=address prog.c`. On Linux, LeakSanitizer runs automatically when the program exits and prints the allocation stack.
3. **macOS:** run `leaks --atExit -- ./prog`.

Key points:
- Here a fixed `char line[256];` on the stack would work better: no allocation, so nothing to leak.

The **height** of a tree is the number of nodes on the longest path from the root down to a leaf. (A **leaf** is a node with no children.) With this definition, an empty tree has height 0. A recursive solution follows directly from it: height = 1 + the larger of the two subtree heights.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

struct tnode {
    int key;
    struct tnode *left, *right;
};

size_t height(const struct tnode *t)
{
    if (t == NULL)
        return 0;
    size_t hl = height(t->left);
    size_t hr = height(t->right);
    return 1 + (hl > hr ? hl : hr);
}

int main(void)
{
    /*      8
           / \
          3   10
         /
        1          */
    struct tnode n1 = {1, NULL, NULL};
    struct tnode n3 = {3, &n1, NULL};
    struct tnode n10 = {10, NULL, NULL};
    struct tnode root = {8, &n3, &n10};

    printf("height = %zu\n", height(&root));   /* 3 */
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- Some textbooks count **edges** instead of nodes. Then a single node has height 0 and an empty tree has height -1. Check which definition your course uses.
- It visits each node once, so it runs in O(n) time.

Same recursive shape. A node with no children counts as 1. Otherwise, add up the leaves in both subtrees.

```c
/* partial snippet: uses struct tnode from above */
size_t count_leaves(const struct tnode *t)
{
    if (t == NULL)
        return 0;
    if (t->left == NULL && t->right == NULL)
        return 1;
    return count_leaves(t->left) + count_leaves(t->right);
}
```

For the example tree, the result is 2 (nodes 1 and 10).

Key points:
- Most tree functions follow this template: handle `NULL`, handle the current node, then combine the results from the left and right subtrees.

A **string builder** keeps three things: a heap buffer, the current length, and the capacity (allocated size). When an append wouldn't fit, it doubles the capacity with `realloc`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

struct strbuf {
    char *data;
    size_t len;   /* chars used, not counting '\0' */
    size_t cap;   /* bytes allocated */
};

int sb_append(struct strbuf *sb, const char *s)
{
    size_t add = strlen(s);
    size_t need = sb->len + add + 1;          /* +1 for '\0' */
    if (need > sb->cap) {
        size_t new_cap = sb->cap ? sb->cap : 16;
        while (new_cap < need)
            new_cap *= 2;
        char *tmp = realloc(sb->data, new_cap);
        if (tmp == NULL)
            return -1;                        /* old data still valid */
        sb->data = tmp;
        sb->cap = new_cap;
    }
    memcpy(sb->data + sb->len, s, add + 1);   /* copies the '\0' too */
    sb->len += add;
    return 0;
}

void sb_free(struct strbuf *sb)
{
    free(sb->data);
    sb->data = NULL;
    sb->len = sb->cap = 0;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct strbuf sb = { NULL, 0, 0 };
    const char *words[] = { "memory ", "is ", "managed ", "by ", "you" };
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof words / sizeof words[0]; i++) {
        if (sb_append(&sb, words[i]) != 0) {
            sb_free(&sb);
            return 1;
        }
    }
    printf("\"%s\" len=%zu cap=%zu\n", sb.data, sb.len, sb.cap);
    sb_free(&sb);
    return 0;
}
```

How it works:
1. It calculates the bytes needed, including the terminator.
2. If they don't fit, it doubles the capacity until they do. Doubling means many appends cost O(1) each on average.
3. It `realloc`s into `tmp`, so a failed allocation doesn't lose the existing text.
4. `memcpy` copies at offset `len`, including the new `'\0'`.

Key points:
- `realloc(NULL, n)` acts like `malloc(n)`, so the empty starting state needs no special case.
- Tracking `len` avoids calling `strlen` on the whole buffer for every append.

An **opaque type** is a struct whose fields are hidden from users of your module. The header only says "this struct exists" (an **incomplete type**). The full definition lives in the `.c` file. Other files can hold pointers to it, but they can't touch its fields, so only your functions can change its data. This is **encapsulation**.

```c
// account.h
#ifndef ACCOUNT_H
#define ACCOUNT_H

typedef struct account account;   /* incomplete: fields hidden */

account *account_new(long start);
int account_withdraw(account *a, long amount);   /* 0 ok, -1 refused */
long account_balance(const account *a);
void account_free(account *a);

#endif
```

```c
// account.c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include "account.h"

struct account {
    long balance;   /* only this file can see this */
};

account *account_new(long start)
{
    account *a = malloc(sizeof *a);
    if (a) a->balance = start;
    return a;
}

int account_withdraw(account *a, long amount)
{
    if (amount < 0 || amount > a->balance) return -1;
    a->balance -= amount;
    return 0;
}

long account_balance(const account *a) { return a->balance; }

void account_free(account *a) { free(a); }
```

```c
// main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "account.h"

int main(void)
{
    account *a = account_new(100);
    if (a == NULL) return 1;
    if (account_withdraw(a, 500) != 0) printf("refused\n");
    account_withdraw(a, 30);
    printf("balance=%ld\n", account_balance(a));   /* 70 */
    /* a->balance = 1000000;  compile error: incomplete type */
    account_free(a);
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- You can change the struct's fields later without changing the header, and callers only need recompiling.
- `FILE` in `<stdio.h>` is used the same way: you only ever hold a `FILE *`.

Because the function needs to change the **caller's** `head` variable, not just the nodes.

C passes everything by value. With `Node *head`, the function gets a copy of the pointer. If it sets `head = new_node`, only the copy changes, and the caller's list still starts at the old node. With `Node **head` (a **pointer to a pointer**), the function gets the address of the caller's variable, so `*head = new_node` updates it.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node { int v; struct Node *next; } Node;

void push_wrong(Node *head, int v)    /* BUG: change is lost */
{
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (!n) return;
    n->v = v; n->next = head;
    head = n;                         /* only the local copy changes */
    free(n);                          /* freed here only so the demo doesn't leak */
}

int push(Node **head, int v)
{
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (!n) return -1;
    n->v = v;
    n->next = *head;
    *head = n;                        /* updates the caller's variable */
    return 0;
}

int main(void)
{
    Node *head = NULL;
    push_wrong(head, 1);
    printf("after push_wrong: %s\n", head ? "not empty" : "empty");
    if (push(&head, 2) != 0) return 1;
    printf("after push: head->v = %d\n", head->v);
    free(head);
    return 0;
}
```

Key points:
- Rule: to change a variable in the caller, pass its address. If the variable is a `Node *`, its address is a `Node **`.
- The alternative is to return the new head (`head = push(head, v);`). Both styles are common.

The extra bytes are **padding**. Many CPUs read an `int` fastest (some can only read it) when its address is a multiple of its **alignment**, usually 4 for a 4-byte `int`. The compiler inserts unused bytes to keep each field aligned.

On a typical 32/64-bit platform:

1. `a` at offset 0 (1 byte), then 3 padding bytes.
2. `b` at offset 4 (4 bytes).
3. `c` at offset 8 (1 byte), then 3 padding bytes at the end, so that in an array of these structs, every element's `b` is aligned too.
4. Total: 12.

Reorder the fields from largest to smallest to reduce padding:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stddef.h>

struct s1 { char a; int b; char c; };
struct s2 { int b; char a; char c; };

int main(void)
{
    printf("s1=%zu s2=%zu\n", sizeof(struct s1), sizeof(struct s2));
    printf("offset of b in s1: %zu\n", offsetof(struct s1, b));
    return 0;
}
```

This typically prints `s1=12 s2=8` and `offset of b in s1: 4`.

Key points:
- Exact padding is **implementation-defined**: it depends on the compiler and CPU. I can't promise 12 on every platform. Use `sizeof` and `offsetof` rather than hard-coding sizes.
- Don't `fwrite` a struct and expect another compiler or machine to read it back the same way.

Push each opening bracket onto a stack. On each closing bracket, pop and check that it matches. At the end, the stack must be empty. This version builds the stack from a linked list, so there's no fixed size limit.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

struct snode { char c; struct snode *next; };

static int push(struct snode **top, char c)
{
    struct snode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (!n) return -1;
    n->c = c;
    n->next = *top;
    *top = n;
    return 0;
}

static bool pop(struct snode **top, char *out)
{
    if (*top == NULL) return false;
    struct snode *n = *top;
    *out = n->c;
    *top = n->next;
    free(n);
    return true;
}

static void clear(struct snode **top)
{
    char dummy;
    while (pop(top, &dummy)) {}
}

static char opener_for(char close)
{
    return close == ')' ? '(' : close == ']' ? '[' : '{';
}

/* 1 = balanced, 0 = not balanced, -1 = out of memory */
int balanced(const char *s)
{
    struct snode *top = NULL;
    for (; *s; s++) {
        if (*s == '(' || *s == '[' || *s == '{') {
            if (push(&top, *s) != 0) { clear(&top); return -1; }
        } else if (*s == ')' || *s == ']' || *s == '}') {
            char open;
            if (!pop(&top, &open) || open != opener_for(*s)) {
                clear(&top);
                return 0;
            }
        }
    }
    int ok = (top == NULL);
    clear(&top);
    return ok;
}

int main(void)
{
    const char *tests[] = { "a[b(c)]{d}", "(]", "((", "" };
    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof tests / sizeof tests[0]; i++)
        printf("\"%s\" -> %d\n", tests[i], balanced(tests[i]));
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `1`, `0`, `0`, `1`.

Key points:
- A closing bracket with an empty stack, a mismatched pair, or leftover openers all mean "not balanced".
- `clear` frees the remaining nodes on every exit path, so an early `return` doesn't leak.

`void *` is a **generic pointer**: it can hold the address of any object type, but you can't dereference it or do arithmetic on it, because C doesn't know the size of what it points to. You supply the size yourself and copy raw bytes with `memcpy`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

/* Swaps two objects of 'size' bytes. Returns 0 on success, -1 on failure. */
int swap_any(void *a, void *b, size_t size)
{
    unsigned char small[64];
    void *tmp = size <= sizeof small ? small : malloc(size);
    if (tmp == NULL)
        return -1;
    memcpy(tmp, a, size);
    memcpy(a, b, size);
    memcpy(b, tmp, size);
    if (tmp != small)
        free(tmp);
    return 0;
}

struct pt { int x, y; };

int main(void)
{
    int i1 = 1, i2 = 2;
    double d1 = 1.5, d2 = 2.5;
    struct pt p1 = {1, 2}, p2 = {3, 4};

    if (swap_any(&i1, &i2, sizeof i1) ||
        swap_any(&d1, &d2, sizeof d1) ||
        swap_any(&p1, &p2, sizeof p1))
        return 1;

    printf("%d %d | %.1f %.1f | (%d,%d)\n", i1, i2, d1, d2, p1.x, p1.y);
    return 0;
}
```

Output: `2 1 | 2.5 1.5 | (3,4)`.

How it works:
1. Any object pointer converts to `void *` automatically, so `&i1`, `&d1` and `&p1` all work.
2. `memcpy` copies `size` bytes regardless of type.
3. It uses a small stack buffer when the object fits, and falls back to `malloc` for big objects.

Key points:
- `void *` throws away type checking. Passing the wrong `size` compiles fine but corrupts memory.
- Don't pass the same object as both `a` and `b`: `memcpy` requires the source and destination not to overlap.
